<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    Linked into the core binary's __TEXT,__info_plist section.

    The bundle names "Keep Vault Launcher" as its main executable, because the
    launcher is what macOS starts and what verifies everything before handing
    over. The process that then actually runs is this binary, which is a
    different file with a different signing identity. macOS resolves a bundle
    from its declared main executable, so for anything that asks "who is this
    process and what is it allowed to do", the running core belongs to no
    bundle at all.

    That matters for TCC. When the scanner helper asks for the camera, the
    request is attributed to the responsible process — this one. Without a
    usage description reachable from it, TCC does not prompt the user, it
    refuses outright. Carrying the description in the binary itself makes the
    core answerable regardless of which Info.plist macOS can or cannot find.
  -->
  <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
  <string>de.michael-feinermann.keep-vault.core</string>
  <key>CFBundleName</key>
  <string>Keep Vault</string>
  <key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
  <string>Keep Vault</string>
  <key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
  <string>APPL</string>
  <key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Keep Vault liest den QR-Code eines gedruckten Schlüsselzettels, damit der 512-Bit-Faktor nicht von Hand eingetippt werden muss. Das Kamerabild wird nur währenddessen ausgewertet und nirgends gespeichert.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
